d (Prevailing), meaning others may collect it, increasing risk if you keep it late. However, since you're not close to winning, better to prioritize building toward high-scoring patterns.\n\nBut looking at your B-tiles: B1, B2, B3\u00d72, B4, B7, B9 \u2014 this is a strong foundation in Bamboos. You could aim for **Full Flush** (24 points) or **Lower Tiles** (24). To progress, keep B1-B4 tightly.\n\nThus, avoid discarding any B1-B4 unless forced. That rules out B1, B2, B3, B4 as discard options right now.\n\nRemaining discard candidates from valid actions: F1, F3, J1, J3, B7, B9, W7, T4.\n\nB7 and B9 are terminals \u2014 less useful for straights unless going for terminal pairs. T4 is isolated. J1 and J3 are Dragons \u2014 keep one set for **All Types**.\n\nHowever, **J3** is White Dragon \u2014 neutral, but again, only one copy.\n\nYou already have one of each Wind and Dragon. Keeping them all would give **All Types** (6 points). But if you must break symmetry, J1 (Red Dragon) is slightly more commonly valued.\n\nBut here\u2019s a key insight: **Greater Honors and Knitted Tiles** requires one of each honor *and* a knitted straight (147+258+369 across suits). You have B1, B4, B7? Yes! B1, B4, B7 \u2014 that\u2019s the full 1-4-7 set in Bamboos. Then you need 2-5-8 in another suit \u2014 you have T4, T5 not present? Wait, do you have T5? No \u2014 hand has T4 only. And 3-6-9 \u2014 B3, B6 (no), B9 \u2014 partial.\n\nWait: B1, B4, B7 \u2014 yes! That\u2019s complete 1-4-7 in Bamboos. Then you need 2-5-8 in Dots or Characters. You have W7 \u2014 not helpful. But no W2, W5, W8. You have B3, B9 \u2014 not helpful.\n\nBut wait: **Knitted Straight** pattern (12 points): one 147, one 258, one 369, plus a meld and a pair. You have B1, B4, B7 \u2014 that\u2019s 147 in Bamboo. Now need 258 and 369 in other suits. You don\u2019t have those. But perhaps build toward it?\n\nAlternatively, **Lesser Honors and Knitted Tiles** (12 points): 14 tiles from the 16: 147, 258, 369, and honors. You have many honors and B1, B4, B7 \u2014 so possible direction.\n\nBut stronger is **Lower Tiles** (24 points): only 1,2,3. You have B1, B2, B3\u00d72, B4 \u2014 B4 is problematic. So discard B4 to move toward Lower Tiles.\n\nSimilarly, B7 and B9 are both high and odd \u2014 not useful for Lower Tiles.\n\nSo best plan: aim for **Lower Tiles** (24 points) by discarding higher-numbered Bamboo tiles: B7, B9, B4.\n\nAmong valid actions: Play B4, Play B7, Play B9.\n\nAlso, you have W7\u00d72 \u2014 could become a pair, but not critical.\n\nT4 is useless \u2014 but not in valid actions? Wait, T4 is in hand and in valid actions \u2014 yes, \u201cPlay T4\u201d is listed.\n\nT4 is completely isolated \u2014 no other T-tiles except T4. Across player has T1-T3, next has T7-T9 \u2014 middle T-tiles are safest? Possibly.\n\nBut T4 is a single suited tile, not helping any major pattern.\n\nSo top discard candidates: T4, B4, B7, B9.\n\nBut **B4** is part of the 1-4-7 knitted possibility \u2014 you already have B1, B4, B7 \u2014 that\u2019s a full 147 set! So discarding B4 breaks **Knitted Straight** potential.\n\nAh! Important: B1, B4, B7 \u2014 you have exactly one of each \u2192 completes the 1-4-7 in Bamboos.\n\nIf you keep that, and get 2-5-8 in say Dots (W2,W5,W8) or Characters (T2,T5,T8), and 3-6-9 in the third, you can make **Knitted Straight** (12 points). Plus you have all four Winds and three Dragons \u2014 that pushes total score up.\n\nTherefore, **keep B1, B4, B7** to preserve **Knitted Straight** option.\n\nThen B9 is useless for that \u2014 3-6-9 would require B3,B6,B9 \u2014 you have B3, B9 \u2014 missing B6. Possible, but B6 unconfirmed.\n\nBut B9 is terminal \u2014 only useful for Outside Hand or All Terminals \u2014 unlikely.\n\nSimilarly, B2 and B3 are useful for Lower Tiles or Chow-building.\n\nSo best discard: **B9** \u2014 it doesn't contribute to 147/258/369 knitted sets (would need B6 for that), and not useful for Lower Tiles unless paired.\n\nWait: you have B3\u00d72 \u2014 so could form pung. B9 is singleton.\n\nAlternatively, **F1** \u2014 East Wind, not your seat wind, prevalent wind \u2014 others may want it, but you have only one \u2014 not building pung.\n\nBut if you discard F1, you lose chance for **All Types**? No \u2014 you can still have one of each \u2014 unless you discard all.\n\nBut **All Types** requires at least one tile from each group: three suits, winds, dragons \u2014 you have all.\n\nDiscarding one honor doesn\u2019t break it \u2014 as long as you retain at least one wind and one dragon.\n\nBut if you discard F1, and later discard F3, then you lose wind representation \u2014 but you still have F1 discarded, but tile exists.\n\nNo \u2014 **All Types** just needs presence in final hand.\n\nSo if you discard F1 now, you no longer have East Wind in hand \u2014 so cannot satisfy **All Types** unless you draw another F1 or recover it \u2014 but only 4 copies, one may be in wall.\n\nUnshown: F1: 3 \u2014 so 3 left \u2014 possible.\n\nBut risky.\n\nBetter to keep one of each until committed.\n\nSame for J1 \u2014 Red Dragon \u2014 discard would break dragon set.\n\nSo best to discard **B9** \u2014 least useful for current strategic paths.\n\nB7 is part of 1-4-7 \u2014 keep.\n\nB4 is part of 1-4-7 \u2014 keep.\n\nB9 is not part of any strong structure.\n\nAlternatively, **T4** \u2014 isolated, no support \u2014 also good discard.\n\nBut T4 could theoretically be part of 258 (if T2,T5,T8) \u2014 but you have none.\n\nWhereas B9 is terminal \u2014 could help Outside Hand (4 points) if you go that way.\n\nBut Lower Tiles (24) > Outside Hand (4).\n\nAnd B9 harms Lower Tiles.\n\nSo discard **B9**.\n\nAnother option: **W7** \u2014 you have two. Could become pung. But W7 is not wind-related. Only value is as suited tile. But not helping any clean pattern.\n\nBut W7\u00d72 could become pair or pung \u2014 2 points for simple pung if terminal/honor \u2014 but W7 is not terminal nor honor? Wait \u2014 W7 is Character-7 \u2014 number 7 \u2014 terminal? No, terminal is 1 or 9.\n\nSo W7 is not terminal \u2014 so Pung of W7 would only count as **All Simples** if rest are 2-8 \u2014 but you have many 1s and 9s.\n\nNot ideal.\n\nSo W7\u00d72 is weak.\n\nBut compared to B9? B9 is worse \u2014 because W7\u00d72 offers pair possibility.\n\nFinal decision: **Play B9** is safest and most logical.\n\nReason: B9 is a terminal tile that does not contribute to strong scoring patterns like Lower Tiles, Knitted Straight, or Full Flush. It hinders pursuit of 24-point patterns such as Lower Tiles. Retaining B1, B2, B3, and B4 supports potential for Lower Tiles or integration into knitted structures. Other discards like F1 or J1 risk breaking future opportunities for All Types (6 points). Thus, discarding B9 minimizes damage to potential high-scoring hands.\n\nAnswer: Play B9"}
